1. The client for the Draw It or Lose it game project is The Gaming Room. The requested software to be developed included server-side and client-side user account management components, secure login methods, and consistent experiences across users and instances. The software was made to support cross-platform support while prioritizing scalability, efficiency, security, and ease of integration.
2. What I believe I did well in the document is being comprehensive and constantly seeking the best option. By analyzing the features different operating systems and platforms offer, I could make a well-educated decision on how to proceed with recommendations.
3. Having a design document was very helpful when developing the code for this project. Having the vision and goals written out, in regular terms, allowed for me to ground myself in the project. Sometimes, coding can get visually messy when you are going at it for hours, and it's important to be able to read the goals instead of analyzing the code.
4. I interprated the user's needs by considering their perspective as a player. I have played many online games before and there are reoccuring issues that I wished to avoid in my design. For example, inconsistent network connection, faulty code that results in bugs (potentially game-breaking). Considering user needs is a huge part of succeeding in design and implementation. Users are the core of the software and ignoring their needs would lead to a abysmal product.
5. To properly understand and implement the client's needs, I followed an approach that is as follows: gathering requirements, outlining for use case analysis, UML diagrams for illustrating relationships, testing and prototyping, and security analysis. In the future, I would love to employ the Agile methodology. Approaching the project as iterations would give me more flexibility in feedback and adapting changes.
